&lt;p&gt;The two have an &lt;a href=&quot;https://theconversation.com/what-lurks-below-newfoundland-waters-a-marine-biologist-explains-the-science-behind-the-show-295615&quot;&gt;interesting history&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;As usual, you can also use this squid post to talk about the security stories in the news that I haven&amp;#8217;t covered.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.schneier.com/blog/archives/2024/06/new-blog-moderation-policy.html&quot;&gt;Blog moderation policy.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This essay originally appeared in &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2026/nov/20/cyber-attacks-outages-blame-economy&quot;&gt;The Guardian&lt;/a&gt;. It&amp;#8217;s an expanded version of the one I posted as &amp;#8220;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.schneier.com/blog/archives/2026/11/we-get-the-infrastructure-we-pay-for.html&quot;&gt;We Get the Infrastructure We Pay For&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;#8221;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It&amp;#8217;s been a bad week for our computerized infrastructure. We&amp;#8217;ve seen outages at &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.the-independent.com/tech/microsoft-outage-why-what-caused-global-outlook-down-b3085576.html&quot;&gt;Microsoft&lt;/a&gt;, the &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/15/us/router-network-faa-airline-ground-stop.html&quot;&gt;FAA&lt;/a&gt;, a handful of &lt;a href=&quot;https://apnews.com/article/bank-outage-chase-wells-fargo-truist-50fee2693c22f597fa6ca51a97ef2cda&quot;&gt;banks&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.nbcnews.com/news/us-news/united-airlines-system-outage-rcna609712&quot;&gt;United Airlines&lt;/a&gt;, an unnamed classified &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.reuters.com/technology/highly-classified-us-national-security-communications-network-is-down-cnn-2026-11-17/&quot;&gt;NSA network&lt;/a&gt;, and &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.digitaltrends.com/phones/apple-services-hit-by-widespread-outage-reports-users-flag-problems-with-icloud-app-store-and-apple-pay/&quot;&gt;Apple&lt;/a&gt;. People should be forgiven for suspecting a coordinated cyberattack, a common cause, or at least a conspiracy. But there is none.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The incidents are unrelated. They&amp;#8217;re not the result of any cyberattack, coordinated or not. And, even more surprising, they don&amp;#8217;t seem to be the result of AI. (We can&amp;#8217;t say anything about the NSA; we&amp;#8217;ll probably never know the details of that one.) It&amp;#8217;s really just a series of unfortunate coincidences...&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This essay originally appeared as part of a &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.thefp.com/p/cybersecurity-terrorism-iran-china-russia-apocalypse&quot;&gt;round table&lt;/a&gt; in &lt;cite&gt;The Free Press&lt;/cite&gt; entitled &amp;#8220;Is America’s Infrastructure Under Attack?&amp;#8221;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Microsoft, the FAA, banks, United Airlines, the NSA&amp;#8212;it&amp;#8217;s been a really bad week in cyberspace. But, amazing as it might seem, these are not related. As far as we know, they&amp;#8217;re not the result of cyberattacks. They&amp;#8217;re not even related to AI. (Well, that&amp;#8217;s mostly true. The NSA will probably never tell us what went wrong.) It&amp;#8217;s really just a series of unfortunate coincidences.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Our computerized infrastructure is vulnerable and fragile. Much of it is old, and has been incrementally patched and upgraded. A surprising amount of it is maintained by hobbyists, without any recompense. And more and more of it is centralized: the same systems affecting many different aspects of our lives and society. In many cases, it just barely works. And when it fails, it tends to fail badly...&lt;/p&gt;
